% Origin: Kowalski, *Computational Logic and Human Thinking*,
%         Chapter 3 "The Fox and the Crow", section 3.5
%         ("The beginning-of-story interpretation as atomic sentences" /
%          "Deriving 'above' from a transitivity rule").
% Last verification: no open issues (no errors/warnings; all tests pass).
%
% The base facts of the story's setting live in the scenario; the single
% transitivity rule in the knowledge base derives further "above" relations.
% In particular "tree is above ground" is DERIVED from "tree above air" and
% "air above ground". To avoid recursion on the same head predicate blocking
% enumeration of derived answers (see fox_and_crow.le note 7), the primitive
% above-facts are stated with "is directly above" and the rule lifts them and
% chains transitively into "is above".

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
*a first object* is directly above *a second object*.
*a first object* is above *a second object*.

the knowledge base above story includes:

% An object is above another if it is directly above it.
a first object is above a second object
    if the first object is directly above the second object.

% Transitivity: one object is above a second object if the first object is
% above a third object and the third object is above the second object.
a first object is above a second object
    if the first object is directly above a third object
    and the third object is above the second object.

scenario story is:
    tree is directly above air.
    air is directly above ground.
    levels expects answers ["tree is above air", "air is above ground", "tree is above ground"].

query levels is:
    which object is above which other object.
